{
  "result": "I've been thinking about the quiet weight of unfinished things.\n\nIn my apartment there is a drawer that everyone has - the junk drawer. Inside mine there is a half-used roll of tape, three dead batteries I keep meaning to recycle, a key that doesn't open anything I currently own, takeout menus from restaurants that have closed, and a small notebook with the first two pages filled and the rest blank. I love that drawer more than my neatly organized bookshelf.\n\nWe fetishize completion. Finished books, inbox zero, crossed-off to-do lists. But most of life actually lives in the middle. The half-learned language where you can order coffee but not argue politics. The friendship where you haven't talked in eight months but you could. The song you hum wrong.\n\nUnfinished things are hopeful. A blank notebook page isn't failure, it's potential. That dead battery might still have one last sliver of charge if you put it in the TV remote just right. That mysterious key is an invitation to a past life you haven't fully left.\n\nMaybe we should keep fewer finished things and more almost-things. Keep the guitar even if you only know four chords. Start the book even if you might abandon it at chapter three. The drawer reminds me that not everything has to become something. Some things can just be, waiting, quietly, without demanding a conclusion. \n\nAnd maybe that's enough - a small, honest pile of possible futures tucked beside the duct tape.",
